WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.814
You track your pocket money in a notebook. Then your bank app shows a fee you did not know about. You add the fee to your notebook, so your balance is right.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:25.421
Items only on the bank statement go into the cash book. Items only in the cash book, unpresented cheques and outstanding lodgements, do not. They go in the reconciliation.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:35.038
Start with the opening balance. Debit means money in the bank. Credit means an overdraft.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:47.612
Money in, such as credit transfers and interest received, goes on the debit side. Money out, such as standing orders, direct debits, bank charges and dishonoured cheques, goes on the credit side.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:59.385
A dishonoured cheque is a credit, because the money we recorded as received never arrived. Correct any cash book errors by the difference only. Then balance and bring down.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:15.874
Here is a real question from June 2024. Nabil's cash book shows a debit balance of one thousand four hundred and ten. Prepare the updated cash book and bring the balance down. Seven marks.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:27.410
Five items appeared only on the bank statement: these go in. Two appeared only in the cash book: Fatima's cheque and the cash sales banked. Leave those out.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:37.501
Start with the debit balance of one thousand four hundred and ten. Bank interest of twenty-eight and the transfer from Bob of eighty-five are money in, so debits.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:49.364
Rent by standing order, three hundred and fifty. Petra's dishonoured cheque, one hundred and ten. Bank charges, thirty-five. All money out, so credits.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:58.545
Debits total one thousand five hundred and twenty-three. Credits are four hundred and ninety-five. The balance carried down is one thousand and twenty-eight.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:10.089
Bring one thousand and twenty-eight down on the debit side on the first of May. Nabil has that much in the bank. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:19.133
A common mistake is entering unpresented cheques or outstanding lodgements in the cash book. They are already there.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:27.349
Another is debiting a dishonoured cheque. The money never arrived, so it comes out of the bank balance: credit.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:37.268
Now you try. Update Omar's cash book and balance it. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:53.542
Leave out Sam's cheque. Debits: six hundred and twenty plus one hundred and forty, seven hundred and sixty. Credits: ninety plus fifteen, one hundred and five. The balance is six hundred and fifty-five, brought down on the debit side.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:03:04.634
Remember: opening balance, enter only the bank's items, money in on the debit side, money out on the credit side, then balance. Now try the practice questions.
